From 1ff6ccd86c76fc5fe8cc299b510f099fc2cfc001 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bernt Budde <bernt.budde@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:34:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 113/119] Tokenizer, Lexer: a format body is one token, and the
 statement ends with it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

perl reads the lines after `format NAME =` as the format's picture and
argument lines, never as code, up to and including the line that holds
a lone . (perlform; toke.c scan_formline):

    printf 'our ($a, $b) = ("x", "y");\nformat STDOUT =\n@<< @>>\n$a, $b\n.\nwrite;\nprint "after\\n";\n' | perl
    # x    y
    # after

A new PPI::Token::Format holds those lines, the . line included, like a
Pod token holds its section.  When the word format starts a statement and
the rest of its line is `[NAME] =`, the new Tokenizer::_format_declaration
makes the tokens of that line itself (Word format, the name as one Word --
perl takes 'one and ::two there -- Operator =) and the next line starts
the Format token; Token::Format adds lines up to the . line.  The Lexer
ends the statement after the Format token, as after a ;, and after it a
statement starts (_label_position, _after_statement_block, _opcontext's
table).  The text of the document is unchanged; format as a hash key, a
method name or a sub name stays a Word.

Removes the PCL §40 TODO marker.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
